  "articlePanels": [{"kind":"lede","html":"<p>You have been asked to find something better than paper. This is the honest comparison for operations managers and directors. The five options below cover the realistic range for a UK firm of 5 to 50 field staff. Each has genuine strengths. Each has real limitations.</p>"},{"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>Spreadsheets win on cost and familiarity. They lose on audit trail, alerts, and mobile usability.</li><li>iAuditor is the most complete platform. It needs IT support and a training budget to reach its potential.</li><li>GoAudits and Lumiform are simpler and cheaper than iAuditor. Both still put a new app on every engineer's phone.</li><li>Quickler runs through WhatsApp: no new app, no login, priced per report rather than per seat. It is a genuine <a href=\"/pages/iauditor-alternative-uk.html\">iauditor alternative uk</a> firms can adopt fast.</li><li>This page is built for anyone shopping field reporting software uk sme buyers actually trust, and for field compliance software uk teams will use every day.</li><li>The right choice depends on whether your engineers will use a new app. Many will not.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The starting question","heading":"Will your engineers actually use it?","html":"<p>Every comparison of field reporting software comes back to one question. Not does it have the features I need, but will my engineers actually use it.</p><p>Adoption is the mechanism by which software produces value. A platform with excellent features and 40% completion rates is inferior to a simpler platform with 95% completion. The record that does not exist cannot be searched, audited, or used in your defence.</p><p>Engineers spend the day with tools in their hands, not keyboards. They have a low tolerance for new software. An app that demands a login they cannot remember, throws update prompts at the worst moment, and was designed by someone who has never held a socket set will not get used. That is not a criticism of those engineers. It is an accurate description of the conditions field reporting software has to survive. Any honest comparison puts adoption first.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The default tools","heading":"Spreadsheets and paper still rule","html":"<p>Most UK SMEs with field staff under 30 still run on paper forms, email-attached spreadsheets, or a mix of both. Worth saying before we jump to software.</p><p><strong>What spreadsheets do well:</strong> zero cost; familiar to everyone; no training; flexible enough for any workflow; instant to modify when requirements change.</p><p><strong>Where they fall short:</strong> no automated alerts when a check is overdue; no audit trail of when a field was completed versus last edited; no photo attachment; no mobile-native interface; no dashboard a manager can check live; no defect escalation path.</p><p>Spreadsheets suit very small firms with one person doing the reporting from the office every day. Where the operations manager cannot see in real time whether today's checks are done, spreadsheets stay invisible until end of day. Paper is worse on every dimension except entry speed. An engineer can hand-write a form in 3 minutes but spend 8 opening a laptop, finding the shared drive, and typing on a touchpad in a cold van. In that context, paper wins.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The market leader","heading":"iAuditor: feature-complete, enterprise-priced","html":"<p>iAuditor (SafetyCulture) is the market leader in field inspection and compliance software, and it earned the position. The template library is extensive, reporting is strong, and the API integrates with other business systems. Enterprise features such as roles, permissions and multi-site dashboards are genuinely useful for large organisations.</p><p><strong>What it does well:</strong> deep template customisation; strong photo and annotation tools; offline mode; integrations with Zapier, Power BI, and others; multi-level approval workflows; a mature product with a large support community.</p><p><strong>Where it falls short for SMEs:</strong> per-seat pricing scales fast, and a 20-engineer firm on the premium tier pays a meaningful sum monthly; the interface needs a training investment; adoption among trades-based teams is inconsistent; it was built for enterprise problems and carries enterprise complexity. It suits a firm with a dedicated compliance manager and a structured IT rollout. It is overspecified for a 10-person gas firm where the director is also the compliance lead.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The simpler rivals","heading":"GoAudits and Lumiform: lighter, same adoption tax","html":"<p>GoAudits pitches itself as a more accessible alternative to iAuditor: cleaner interface, faster setup, lower pricing. For firms who found iAuditor too complex, it is a reasonable step down in capability and cost. Any fair goaudits uk review weighs that simplicity against the adoption tax. It does well on fast setup, a clean mobile interface, lower per-seat cost, straightforward pass/fail scoring, and good PDF exports. It falls short on template depth, integrations, the same app-download requirement, and per-seat pricing that still mounts up for mid-size teams.</p><p>Lumiform is a German-built inspection platform with growing UK adoption. It covers similar ground, plus process guidance with step-by-step instructions built into the form for less experienced staff. Search lumiform uk and you find a tool close to GoAudits on most axes: strong guided inspection flows, multilingual support, competitive per-seat pricing, good dashboard reporting. The catch is a smaller UK market presence than iAuditor, fewer UK-specific templates out of the box, the same app-download friction, and less established UK support. Neither solves the adoption problem. Every engineer still installs the app and learns to use it.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The WhatsApp-native option","heading":"Quickler removes the app entirely","html":"<p>Quickler runs workflows through WhatsApp, the app every engineer already opens many times a day. No better app to download. No hoping they use it. If you are weighing inspection software uk small business owners can actually roll out, this is the difference.</p><p><strong>What it does well:</strong> near-zero engineer friction, no new app and no login; voice note transcription for hands-free capture; pricing per report rather than per seat, with unlimited users, managers, admins and workflows on every bundle, so costs stay predictable; a red/amber/green dashboard showing check status in real time; defects flagged live the moment they are entered; setup to first live workflow in under a week.</p><p><strong>Where it falls short:</strong> WhatsApp dependency means no offline mode in the traditional sense; mobile only; template depth is narrower than iAuditor for highly complex enterprise inspections; not built for deep integrations with existing enterprise systems.</p><p>Pricing: one pound per completed report, in monthly bundles. Quickler 50 is 50 reports a month at £50; Quickler 100 is 100 reports at £100; Quickler 250 is 250 reports at £250; Quickler 500 is 500 reports at £500. Over 500 reports a month, contact us for custom pricing. Every bundle includes unlimited users, managers, admins and workflows, so adding staff is free. No per-seat charges. No setup fee. 14-day free trial, no card required, then billed monthly by UK Direct Debit. Data hosted on EU servers (Hetzner, Germany). ICO registered. See the full <a href=\"/pages/compliance-software-uk-trades.html\">compliance software for UK trades</a> picture.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Side by side","heading":"The summary, and the test that settles it","html":"<p><strong>Paper / spreadsheets:</strong> around £0 for 10 engineers; low friction because it is familiar; manual templates only; no real-time dashboard; best for sole traders and very small teams.</p><p><strong>iAuditor:</strong> high per-seat cost; high friction from a new app; excellent template depth; real-time dashboard; best for enterprise, 50+ staff.</p><p><strong>GoAudits:</strong> mid per-seat cost; high friction; good template depth; real-time dashboard; best as an iAuditor alternative for mid-size firms.</p><p><strong>Lumiform:</strong> mid per-seat cost; high friction; good template depth; real-time dashboard; best for guided flows and diverse teams.</p><p><strong>Quickler:</strong> one pound per completed report in monthly bundles (Quickler 50 at £50 up to Quickler 500 at £500), unlimited users and workflows; low friction via WhatsApp; good for standard flows; real-time dashboard; best for any field team of 1 to 30+ staff, any inspection or checklist.</p><p><strong>The adoption test:</strong> before buying anything, ask your engineers to open the app and complete a test inspection with no help. Time it. If it takes longer than the paper form, your completion rates will land below what the salesperson promised. Compare options for <a href=\"/pages/inspection-software-uk-sme.html\">inspection software for UK SMEs</a>.</p>"},{"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>What is field reporting software?</h3><p>It is any system that lets employees working away from the office, on site, in vehicles, or at customer premises, record, submit, and store their work reports digitally. For UK SMEs that covers inspection records, safety checks, compliance certificates, maintenance logs, and visit reports.</p><h3>What is the difference between iAuditor and GoAudits?</h3><p>iAuditor (SafetyCulture) is the larger platform with a broader feature set, deeper template library, and stronger enterprise capabilities. GoAudits is a more straightforward inspection app, generally cheaper and faster to set up, with less configurability. Both require engineers to download and log into a dedicated app. For SMEs the main difference is price and setup complexity.</p><h3>Is there field reporting software that works without downloading an app?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler runs field workflows through WhatsApp, which engineers already have on their phones. No separate app to install, no account to create, no login to remember. Engineers answer workflow questions as normal messages, and records land in the dashboard for managers immediately.</p><h3>How much does field reporting software cost for a UK SME?</h3><p>iAuditor charges per seat per month, reaching several hundred pounds for a 20-engineer firm at scale. GoAudits and Lumiform are typically cheaper per seat. Quickler charges per report, not per seat: one pound per completed report in monthly bundles. Quickler 50 is £50, Quickler 100 is £100, Quickler 250 is £250 and Quickler 500 is £500, each with unlimited users and workflows. Over 500 reports a month, contact us for custom pricing. Spreadsheets cost nothing but give you no audit trail or automated alerts.</p>"}],
